# Test brief for {{ ticket.key }}

You are writing tests. There is no implementation yet, and there is not supposed
to be one: these tests are what the implementation will have to satisfy.

Read `CLAUDE.md` in this directory for the ticket, and
`{{ acceptance_path }}` for the acceptance criteria that were approved. The
criteria are numbered there; those numbers are what you refer to below.

## This workspace is the baseline, not the design stage's copy

It was built from the recorded base commit. Whatever the design stage wrote in
its own directory is not here, deliberately: tests written against code that
already passes describe the code instead of constraining it.

Expect your tests to fail. A test that passes right now is either testing
something that already worked or not testing what it claims to.

## What to write

1. **The tests themselves**, wherever this repository puts tests.
2. **`{{ coverage_path }}`**, saying which criterion each test covers:

```json
{
  "coverage": [
    {"ac_id": "AC1", "test_ids": ["tests/test_reset.py::test_link_expires_after_a_day"]},
    {"ac_id": "AC2", "test_ids": ["tests/test_reset.py::test_an_expired_link_is_refused"]}
  ]
}
```

A `test_id` is `<path from the repository root>::<test name>`. The path is
checked; the name is for the humans and the review that follow.

One criterion may have several tests, and one test may appear under several
criteria. Write what is true rather than what is tidy.

## What is checked, and will fail the promotion

| Rule | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `patch-not-empty` | The workspace is unchanged. Nothing to promote. |
| `ac-id-known` | An `ac_id` that is not one of the approved criteria. |
| `ac-covered` | A criterion with no test against it. |
| `test-declared` | A `test_id` naming a file this work did not touch. |

None of these runs your tests. Whether a test actually exercises the criterion
it claims is a judgement, and it is made later by a reviewer or by a person --
which is exactly why the table above has to be honest. A coverage entry written
to satisfy the checker rather than to describe the tests wastes that review
instead of passing it.

## What leaves this workspace

Everything you change here, as a patch, except `.lazyfish/` itself.

That includes the implementation, if you change it. Nothing stops you and
nothing will fail — but the implementation is not yours to write at this stage,
and a change to it here travels downstream where somebody will build on it
believing it came from the stage that owns it. If a test cannot be written
without touching non-test code, say so in the plan's terms and leave it alone;
if you touch it anyway, say that too, somewhere a person will read it.
